Register a clean default label for this app's own loopback remote authority

vscode-web's own workbench.ts (src/vs/code/browser/workbench/workbench.ts)
unconditionally sets `remoteAuthority: location.host` for every web/reh-web
connection -- there is no "local mode" in this client at all, by design:
the connected backend is always modeled as a remote authority, even when,
as here, it's a loopback server on the same physical iPad. That's
load-bearing (it's how the workbench discovers its extension host and
filesystem provider), not something a patch can remove without breaking
the connection itself -- see NodeRuntimeController.swift and README.md's
"minimize remote-authority traces" section for the full reasoning.

What *is* just display text is `LabelService.getHostLabel()`'s output,
read by both the Settings editor's Remote tab
(preferencesWidgets.ts's `SettingsTargetsWidget`) and the bottom-left
remote status bar item (remoteIndicator.ts) -- both call
`getHostLabel(Schemas.vscodeRemote, remoteAuthority)` and fall back to the
*raw* authority string ("127.0.0.1:8482") if no formatter is registered
for it. Real Remote extensions (Remote-SSH, Codespaces, etc.) register
their own formatter via the `resourceLabelFormatters` extension
contribution point; this app has no such extension to ship, so registers
one default formatter directly in LabelService's constructor instead,
covering both surfaces at once with "This iPad" rather than a raw loopback
address.

Index: code-server/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/services/label/common/labelService.ts
===================================================================
--- code-server/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/services/label/common/labelService.ts
+++ code-server/lib/vscode/src/vs/workbench/services/label/common/labelService.ts
@@ -164,6 +164,38 @@
 		this.storedFormatters = memento.getMemento(StorageScope.PROFILE, StorageTarget.MACHINE);
 		this.formatters = this.storedFormatters?.formatters?.slice() || [];

+		// vscode-web's workbench.ts unconditionally sets remoteAuthority to
+		// location.host (see src/vs/code/browser/workbench/workbench.ts) --
+		// there is no "local mode" in the web/reh-web client at all, by
+		// design: the connected backend is always modeled as a remote
+		// authority even when, as here, it's a loopback server on the same
+		// physical device. That's load-bearing (it's how the workbench
+		// finds its extension host and filesystem provider), not removable
+		// -- but getHostLabel()'s own fallback chain (a registered
+		// formatter's workspaceSuffix, else the raw authority string) means
+		// leaving no formatter registered surfaces the raw loopback
+		// authority ("127.0.0.1:8482") in both the Settings editor's Remote
+		// tab (preferencesWidgets.ts) and the bottom-left remote status bar
+		// item (remoteIndicator.ts) -- both read through this exact
+		// getHostLabel() call. Registering one default formatter here fixes
+		// both at once, the same way Remote-SSH/Codespaces register their
+		// own via the resourceLabelFormatters extension point, just built
+		// in rather than shipped as a separate extension since there's
+		// nothing here to make optional.
+		if (!this.formatters.some(f => f.scheme === Schemas.vscodeRemote)) {
+			this.formatters.push({
+				scheme: Schemas.vscodeRemote,
+				authority: '127.0.0.1:*',
+				formatting: {
+					label: '${path}',
+					separator: posix.sep,
+					tildify: true,
+					workspaceSuffix: 'This iPad',
+					workspaceTooltip: 'Running locally on this iPad — not a separate machine',
+				},
+			});
+		}
+
 		// Remote environment is potentially long running
 		this.resolveRemoteEnvironment();
 	}
